hey everyone this is kevin from  thechesswebsite.com and today we're  going to be going over the most popular  variations  in the queen's gambit so many people  interested not only in chess these days  but  in the queen's gambit opening which  starts out with d4  d5 and c4 so i thought it'd be a good  video  to go over the main variations that  you'll see  that black can actually play after white  plays  c4 i do have in-depth videos  on all the variations i'm going to cover  so if you ever want to check those out  you can head over to thechesswebsite.com  and check those that'll also have a link  in the description below to  my website but there's really four main  variations that you'll see  from black in the queen's gambit there's  two others that we'll discuss if you're  trying to catch your opponent off guard  but the first and most popular defense  in the queen's gambit is for black to  play  c6 this is the slob defense this is not  taking the pawn here on c4 that's the  accepted line  but to defend this central pawn here on  d5 white's really looking to give up  material here on c4  so that white now has the two central  pawns black  only has one central pawn here on e on  the e  file has given up his central pawn to  gain that material here  and the slav defense says that's not  what we're looking to try to do  we want to play c6 solidify  this pawn chain right here and protect  the central pawn  here on d5 most common line for white is  going to be to continue with knight to  f3  black plays knight to f6 and then white  has a couple options if  white plays e3 then black can play  bishop to  g4 and this is the the main slav line  you could also get into the semi-slav  line which is e6  this is blocking the light squared  bishop but solidifying the pawn chain  right here  it can become difficult for the light  square bishop to come outside this pawn  chain once  this pawn here is on e6 so some people  kind of have a preference if they play  the slav  or the semislav  the other defense for black that's just  as popular is the slob defense is the  queen's gambit orthodox line and that is  with  e6 solidifying the pawn here on d5  also preparing for the dark square  bishop to come outside  and attack the king prepare for a quick  castle on  the king side play normally resumes with  knight to c3  knight to f6 bishop down to g5  pinning down the knight to the queen  getting the bishop outside the pawn  chain before white plays pawn to e3  and you could see bishop to e7 stopping  the pin  e3 and then castle on the king side but  black has options to be tricky instead  of bishop to e7 could always play knight  to d7 getting into the elephant trap  which  there's another video i've done on that  trap you can definitely check that out  on the website but i'll go through it  real quick  pawn takes here on d5 pawn recaptures on  d5  white takes on d5 and it looks like why  would black  ever take this knight he's gonna lose  his queen doesn't matter that's exactly  what happens  after the bishop takes here bishop to b4  and you can see  check and the only way to not lose the  game  is to play queen to d2 so after the  bishop takes  king to d2 king takes here on  d8 and black is up a ton of material  coming in at the third most popular  variation is the accepted line and  that's if  black takes here on a c4 now the queen's  gambit not your typical gambit  black's going to gain material here on a  c4 but white can always get that  material back  black usually will just give the  material on c4 back  pretty quickly because if black does not  they're really going to ruin their  entire position  trying to hold on to that pawn so play  will typically resume with knight to f3  you could see knight to f6 pawn here to  e3  preparing for the bishop to come and  take here on c4 get that material back  here  pawn e6 bishop takes here on c4  and then pawn c5 since white has  the two pawns in the center of the board  black only has one  black using that c pawn which white  early on used their c  pawn to kind of attack the center of the  board that's exactly what black does  here and you can see the bishop on  f8 is protecting this pawn so if there  ever was an exchange  the bishop could take that with tempo  now they don't have to  play knight to f3 they could just play  something like e3 right away  so if they just want to capture that  material on at the next move they  absolutely could  there's four main variations that you'll  see in the queen's game but the fourth  one we're going to be looking at  not as popular as the other three but  definitely still we'll see it played and  that is the albin counter gambit and  that is  e5 here now i'm going to make a  reference to  the netflix series the queen's game but  if you haven't seen that i definitely  don't want to spoil anything for you  i'd recommend skipping forward about two  minutes in this video  that should make sure you don't catch  any spoilers but in the very last  game in that series you have beth harmon  playing borghoff for the world  championship beth harmon starts out with  the queen's gambit here  and borgob usually plays pretty  straightforward lines plays the move  e5 plays the albin counter gambit now  you hear that  said in the series and so this should  give you a better idea of what was  actually  played now the alvin kenner argument is  pretty tricky after the pawn takes on e5  which is undefended black plays the move  d4  this is a central wedge keeping these  pawns away from each other here and it's  difficult for white to deal with  the white's normally wanting to play  moves like knight to c3 which they can't  do  and so it's black has a lot of threats  that white has to deal with  you could play the the alaska trap so  maybe they try to play  uh e3 since they already have a central  pawn here on the e file they could try  to just chip away at this pawn  but then things like bishop to b4 are  tricky they can't play knight to c3 as  we talked about so  bishop two d2 and you get into this  weird trap  uh where check and then they can't take  the pawn because you could just take  their queen  and if they play e2 you could just under  promote to  a knight here and things get pretty  tricky there's another video i've done  on the alaska  trap all in all definitely not where  white would want to be  they just have to be aware of those  things now the main line instead of pawn  to e3  is going to just be knight to f3 uh  knight to c6 so  even as you're watching uh that last  match you can start to see  how this shapes up so when you see the  board you'll see okay i understand how  they got there  there are two more variations that  you'll see from time to time  uh they're both the chagoran as well as  the baltic  neither of these see a ton of play and  most times people will use them as  a surprise attack because the queen's  game is so popular for white  the score in defense is knight to c6  and then you have the baltic which is  bishop here to  f5 not going to go too much into the  theory on either of these i do have a  video on  both of those openings on the websites  definitely check those out if you want  to but hopefully you enjoyed  this video going over the main  variations  hopefully if you haven't spent too much  time on the queen's game but this gives  you a better understanding of the  options that black has if they're ever  met with the queen's gambit from white  if you haven't already please hit the  like button definitely helps  support the channel let me know what  your favorite variation in the queen's  game it is below  thank you guys so much watching the  video and i'll see you next time bye  you

